{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:4075",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the relationship with Enolase-phosphatase E1 through a multi-step mechanistic pathway. The evidence suggests that benzocaine acts as a target for a compound, 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, which is a known target of the tumor susceptibility gene 101 protein. This compound, in turn, targets Enolase-phosphatase E1, indicating a potential pathway involving the tumor susceptibility gene 101 protein as a mediator. Similarly, benzocaine is also linked to the glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase [isomerizing] enzyme, which is also targeted by the same compound, reinforcing the role of 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline in the pathway. The presence of multiple targets, including the tumor susceptibility gene 101 protein and the glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase, suggests a broader biological context where benzocaine may influence cellular processes through this compound. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for benzocaine to modulate Enolase-phosphatase E1 through the action of 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and mechanism of action.",
